+## Decision 1 — License and first-party notice scope
+
+**Measured facts.** The generated package table distinguishes each `license` field from an actual package-local LICENSE file. The repository has an MIT LICENSE, copied into the private bundle; some private workspaces have no license field. Shipping that existing notice under Gate 1 did not settle Gate 2's license choice. The bundle's third-party closure has its own package notices and SBOM; a first-party choice must not relabel those dependencies.
+
+**Options and consequences.** Continue private Gate 1 delivery with the existing notices while leaving the choice open; this preserves the current boundary and postpones public license normalization. Alternatively, Derek can explicitly ratify MIT for a named first-party release package set, followed by a scoped metadata/notice change and dependency-notice verification. Choosing different terms requires a separately specified first-party scope and review of the rights needed for that distribution. Neither a missing field nor an existing `publishConfig` is permission to choose for him.
+
+**One-way-door status.** No new license decision has been made here. Source metadata can be edited later, but publicly distributed copies and the terms accompanying them cannot be treated as recalled merely by changing the repository.
+
+MIT's published terms require preservation of the copyright and permission notice in copies or substantial portions. That notice obligation is distinct from approving a new release or establishing ownership of every bundled input. [MIT license text](https://opensource.org/license/mit).
+
+**Proposed decision text, pending Derek:** “Retain the existing license fields and LICENSE files for this private release proof. Gate 2 license approval remains pending. Before any public release, present the exact first-party package set and proposed terms for an explicit decision; preserve the third-party notices and closure evidence independently. This disposition grants no publication permission.”
+
+## Decision 2 — Public npm distribution and MCP Registry metadata
+
+**Measured facts.** The package table exposes publication-shaped workspaces, private workspaces and their actual entry/export/file declarations. The root package is a repository workspace, not a measured standalone installer; `private:true` is the authorized safeguard. #1632 and the s181/s182 record say Derek claimed `@oods` with zero publishes at that time. That is a dated decision record, not a fresh npm account inventory. No publication is performed by this mission, and it prepares no MCP Registry `server.json` or public installer. The generated package and runtime facts do not establish publication history outside this repository.
+
+The official MCP Registry hosts server metadata pointing to a distribution location; it does not replace a package registry. Its public registry does not support a private, narrow-audience installation method. A private Gate 1 tarball therefore is not itself a justified public listing. [Official registry overview](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/registry/about).
+
+**Options and consequences.** Keep named private handoffs, with the existing digest/runbook workflow and no registry discovery. Or choose a public installer channel, then select its exact package identity, ownership, version, payload, update policy and release operator before preparing a listing. npm is one possible channel, not an assumed adoption interface. Publishing every workspace would create additional versioned public boundaries; the measured self-contained runtime layout does not establish that each workspace is independently installable as a public product.
+
+**One-way-door status.** A local package candidate and metadata draft are reversible preparation. Public artifact distribution and indexing expose copies and create an installation contract. No such door is opened by this packet or by existing public-access metadata.
+
+npm permits unpublishing only under its stated conditions, and a published package name/version pair cannot be reused even after removal. This is a concrete versioning consequence, not a claim that deletion is always impossible. [npm unpublish policy](https://docs.npmjs.com/policies/unpublish/).
+
+**Proposed decision text, pending Derek:** “Keep public npm publication and MCP Registry submission deferred. Evaluate one exact public installer candidate after the final frozen-head bundle and independent review exist. The later authorization must name the package/channel, version, payload digest, publisher and license decision; it must not grant blanket publication of workspace packages.”
